  {
   "title": "Power in a balanced load",
   "body": "Add the three phase powers. With rms line quantities the result is the same for wye and delta loads. $\\theta$ is the angle of the **load impedance** (the angle between phase voltage and phase current), not the 30° between line and phase quantities.\n**Pitfall:** $\\sqrt3 V_L I_L$ uses line values; $3V_YI_L$ uses the phase voltage. Never multiply by both 3 and $\\sqrt3$.",
   "formula": "P = \\sqrt3\\,V_L I_L\\cos\\theta,\\quad Q = \\sqrt3\\,V_L I_L\\sin\\theta,\\quad |S| = \\sqrt3\\,V_LI_L"
  },
  {
   "title": "Per-phase analysis",
   "body": "To solve any balanced system: convert Δ sources and loads to Y ($V_Y = V_L/\\sqrt3$, $Z_Y = Z_\\Delta/3$), imagine a neutral wire joining the star points, and solve **phase a only**. The other phases have the same magnitudes, shifted by −120° and +120°. Multiply phase powers by 3.",
   "formula": "\\mathbf I_{aA} = \\frac{\\mathbf V_{an}}{Z_{line} + Z_Y}",
