     "id": "q2",
     "type": "trueFalse",
     "prompt": "In a balanced wye–wye system, the current in the neutral wire is zero.",
     "answer": true,
     "explanation": "$\\mathbf I_{Nn} = \\mathbf I_{aA} + \\mathbf I_{bB} + \\mathbf I_{cC}$: three phasors of equal size, 120° apart. Their sum is $I_L\\angle{-\\theta}\\,(1 + 1\\angle{-120^\\circ} + 1\\angle 120^\\circ) = I_L\\angle{-\\theta}\\,(1 - 0.5 - 0.5) = 0$. So in a balanced system the neutral can be removed without changing anything."
    },
    {
     "id": "i2",
     "type": "info",
     "title": "Line-to-line voltages",
     "body": "The voltage between two lines follows from KVL: $\\mathbf V_{ab} = \\mathbf V_{an} - \\mathbf V_{bn}$. Working this out gives a phasor $\\sqrt3$ times larger that **leads** $\\mathbf V_{an}$ by 30°. Likewise $\\mathbf V_{bc}$ leads $\\mathbf V_{bn}$ by 30°, and $\\mathbf V_{ca}$ leads $\\mathbf V_{cn}$ by 30°.",
     "formula": "\\mathbf V_{ab} = \\sqrt3\\,\\mathbf V_{an}\\angle 30^\\circ,\\qquad V_L = \\sqrt3\\,V_Y"
